{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Ogilvie Raceway, is a 4/10 mile Dirt clay oval race track located near Ogilvie, Minnesota.","title":"Ogilvie Raceway"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Named after the nearby town Ogilvie.","title":"Ogilvie Raceway"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It hosts a weekly Wissota racing program consisting of Modifieds, Super Stocks, Midwest Modifieds, Modified 4sStreet Stocks and Hornets.","title":"Ogilvie Raceway"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"USCGC "Papaw" (WLB-308) was a sea-going buoy tender whose design is based on the pre-World War II United States Lighthouse Service Tenders.","title":"USCGC Papaw (WLB-308)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The original design was modified to provide an armored cutter capable of wartime missions in addition to her primary mission of Aids to Navigation.","title":"USCGC Papaw (WLB-308)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" "Papaw" was built in 1943 by the Marine Iron and Shipbuilding Company of Duluth, Minnesota.","title":"USCGC Papaw (WLB-308)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Commissioned 12 October 1943, she was assigned the home port of San Francisco, California.","title":"USCGC Papaw (WLB-308)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The St. Peter Armory is a former National Guard Armory in St. Peter, Minnesota, United States.","title":"St. Peter Armory"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Built from 1912 to 1913, it is one of the oldest armories still standing in Minnesota and was the first one to be owned by the state upon its completion.","title":"St. Peter Armory"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It was designed in a restrained Gothic Revival style which captures a transition in Minnesota armory design from heavy, fortresslike buildings to the simpler designs of the 1920s.","title":"St. Peter Armory"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1997 for having local significance in the themes of architecture, military history, and social history.","title":"St. Peter Armory"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" It was nominated for being an excellent example of Minnesota's pre-World War I armories, as well as for its early status, transitional architecture, and role in St. Peter as a center not only of military affairs but also social and recreational events.","title":"St. Peter Armory"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"De Lysle Ferrée Cass (1887–1973) was a writer of fantasy short stories.","title":"De Lysle Ferree Cass"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He had at least six stories published pre-World War I in magazines from the Munsey pulp stable.","title":"De Lysle Ferree Cass"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" These include: "Oahula the Carnivorous" ("The All-Story" March 1913); "Pilgrims in Love" ("The All-Story" Sep and Oct 1913); "The Love Caprice" ("The All-Story" November 1913); "Love Goes Blindly" ("The All-Story" Dec 1913); "The Man Who Could Not Die" ("All Story" March 1914 and "The All-Story" Mar 7, 1914); and "The White Spot" ("All-Story Cavalier Weekly", Oct 24 and 31, 1914).","title":"De Lysle Ferree Cass"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" His stories for the Munsey pulps are marked by a frank eroticism unusual for its time, together with frequent settings in Oriental climes.","title":"De Lysle Ferree Cass"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Germany Olympic football team represents Germany in international football competitions in Olympic Games.","title":"Germany Olympic football team"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It has been active since 1908, and first competed in 1912.","title":"Germany Olympic football team"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Olympic football was originally an amateur sport, and as the pre-World War II German national team was also amateur, it was able to send a full national team to the games.","title":"Germany Olympic football team"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" After the war, Germany was divided, but until 1964 East and West competed under the name of "United Team of Germany", although without a combined squad.","title":"Germany Olympic football team"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" From 1968 West Germany began to compete on its own, but were still forced to send an amateur team, who were not able to match the success of their professional counterparts in the World Cup and European Championship.","title":"Germany Olympic football team"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The rules on amateurism were relaxed in the 1980s, which allowed West Germany some success, notably a bronze medal finish in 1988.","title":"Germany Olympic football team"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Since 1992 the tournament has been competed by under-23 teams, making Germany's Olympic qualification dependent on the results of the under-21 team.","title":"Germany Olympic football team"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" Only in 2016 the Germans returned to the Olympic stage, with a silver medal after losing on a penalty shoot-out to hosts Brazil.","title":"Germany Olympic football team"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" Reunified Germany is now the only World Cup champion without the Olympic gold.","title":"Germany Olympic football team"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Lore Kruger (1914–2009) was a German-Jewish photographer and Nazi resistor, known most notably for the photographs taken in pre-World War II Europe.","title":"Lore Kruger"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Born in Magdeburg, Germany, as Lore Heinemann on March 11, 1914, Kruger was part of a Jewish-German family.","title":"Lore Kruger"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" She grew up taking photographs for fun and soon it became her career.","title":"Lore Kruger"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" As her family fled from rising Nazi sentiment in Germany, Lore Kruger and her camera captured emigration and resistance in turbulent pre-World War II Europe.","title":"Lore Kruger"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Ogilvie
