{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Bologna Process is a series of ministerial meetings and agreements between European countries to ensure comparability in the standards and quality of higher-education qualifications.","title":"Bologna Process"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The process has created the European Higher Education Area under the Lisbon Recognition Convention.","title":"Bologna Process"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It is named after the University of Bologna, where the Bologna declaration was signed by education ministers from 29 European countries in 1999.","title":"Bologna Process"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The process was opened to other countries in the European Cultural Convention of the Council of Europe, and governmental meetings have been held in Prague (2001), Berlin (2003), Bergen (2005), London (2007) and Leuven (2009).","title":"Bologna Process"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The European Master's in Translation (EMT) is a partnership project between the "Directorate-General for Translation" (DGT) of the "European Commission" and a number of universities from a wide range of European countries.","title":"European Master's in Translation"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" EMT is a quality label for translation programmes that offer a Master's degree.","title":"European Master's in Translation"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The DGT awards the label to higher education programmes that meet the EMT quality standards for translator training.","title":"European Master's in Translation"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Master programmes that meet these standards can become members of the EMT network.","title":"European Master's in Translation"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The initial project was launched in 2006 and the first network was set up in December 2009, following a rigorous selection in which applicant university programmes were evaluated by academic assessors.","title":"European Master's in Translation"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" This first EMT Network consisted of 34 universities in 16 European countries.","title":"European Master's in Translation"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" In 2011, 20 more programmes were selected, bringing the total number of members to 54 programmes in 20 European countries.","title":"European Master's in Translation"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" In the most recent selection round in 2014, EMT membership increased to 63 member programmes from 22 countries, including two non-EU members (from Switzerland).","title":"European Master's in Translation"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" The project serves as a model in its approach to convergence in higher education in Europe, in the broad context of the Bologna process.","title":"European Master's in Translation"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Swedish Higher Education Act is the Swedish law governing higher education in Sweden.","title":"Swedish Higher Education Act"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Around 80% of the funding for higher education in Sweden is provided by the government.","title":"Swedish Higher Education Act"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The Swedish higher education system is a part of the Bologna Process.","title":"Swedish Higher Education Act"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Education in Cambodia is controlled by the state through the Ministry of Education in a national level and by the Department of Education at the provincial level.","title":"Education in Cambodia"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The Constitution of Cambodia establishes that the state shall protect and upgrade citizen's rights to quality education at all levels, guaranteeing that all citizens have equal opportunity to earn a living (Article 66).","title":"Education in Cambodia"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The state shall adopt an education program "according to the principle of modern pedagogy including technology and foreign languages," as well as the state controls public and private schools and classrooms at all levels (Article 67).","title":"Education in Cambodia"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The Cambodian education system includes pre-school, primary, general secondary, higher education and non-formal education.","title":"Education in Cambodia"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The education system includes the development of sport, information technology education, research development and technical education.","title":"Education in Cambodia"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" School enrollment has increased during the 2000s in Cambodia.","title":"Education in Cambodia"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" USAID data shows that in 2011 primary enrollment reached 96% of the child population, lower secondary school 34% and upper secondary 21%.","title":"Education in Cambodia"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Higher education in Pakistan is the systematic process of students continuing their education beyond secondary school, learned societies, and two-year colleges.","title":"List of universities in Pakistan"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The governance of higher education is maintained under the Higher Education Commission (HEC) which oversees the financial funding, research outputs, and teaching quality in the country.","title":"List of universities in Pakistan"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In Pakistan, the higher education system includes the public, private, military, and vocational universities, all accredited by the HEC.","title":"List of universities in Pakistan"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Since independence, new universities have expanded throughout the country with support provided by the University Grants Commission (UGC), which had been an autonomous institution of recognizing universities until 2002 when it was preceded by the HEC.","title":"List of universities in Pakistan"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Pakistan produces about 445,000 university graduates and 10,000 computer science graduates annually.","title":"List of universities in Pakistan"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" A number of institutions of higher learning are active in the country, but the HEC recognizes 183 institutions.","title":"List of universities in Pakistan"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" This article provides a comprehensive list of higher education institutions active in Pakistan.","title":"List of universities in Pakistan"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Higher education in Japan is provided at universities (大学 "daigaku"), junior colleges (短期大学 "tanki daigaku"), colleges of technology (高等専門学校 "kōtō senmon gakkō") and special training schools and community colleges (専修学校 "senshū gakkō").","title":"Higher education in Japan"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Of these four types of institutions, only universities and junior colleges are strictly considered postsecondary education providers.","title":"Higher education in Japan"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The modern Japanese higher education system has undergone numerous changes since the Meiji period and was largely modeled after Western countries such as Germany, France, Britain, and the United States to create a unique Japanese model to serve its national needs.","title":"Higher education in Japan"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The Japanese higher education system differs from higher education in most other countries in many significant ways.","title":"Higher education in Japan"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Key differences include the method of acceptance, which relies almost entirely on one or two tests, as opposed to GPAs (Grade Point Average) or other methods of assessment used in Western countries.","title":"Higher education in Japan"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Because students only have one chance to take this test each year, there is an enormous amount of pressure to do well on this test, and the majority of senior high school education is dedicated to doing well on this single test.","title":"Higher education in Japan"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Bologna Process
